    <h3>Will a free VIN check show accidents?</h3>
    <p>No, free VIN checks do not show accident history. Accident records are maintained by insurance companies, repair shops, and state DMVs—not by the NHTSA databases that free VIN checks access. To see accident history, you'll need a paid vehicle history report from services like Carfax or AutoCheck. However, free VIN checks do show critical safety information including recalls, which is often more immediately relevant than accident history. For guidance on when you need paid reports, see our comparison guide: <a href="/guides/carfax-vs-vin-lookup/">Carfax vs. VIN Lookup</a>.</p>

    <h3>How current is the recall data?</h3>
    <p>The NHTSA recall database is updated continuously as manufacturers report new recalls. When you run a free VIN check through Car Alpha, you're accessing the most current recall data available at that moment. Recalls are added to the database within days of manufacturer announcement. We recommend checking your VIN quarterly to catch new recalls as they're issued, since safety defects are often discovered years after vehicles are sold.</p>

    <h3>What vehicles can I check?</h3>
    <p>You can check any vehicle manufactured for sale in the United States since the VIN system was standardized in 1981. This includes cars, trucks, SUVs, motorcycles, RVs, and commercial vehicles. The NHTSA database covers all manufacturers selling vehicles in the US, including domestic brands, imports, and specialty manufacturers. Vehicles manufactured before 1981 used non-standardized VINs and may not return complete results.</p>

    <h3>Do I need to create an account?</h3>
    <p>No account is required to use Car Alpha's free VIN check. Simply enter a VIN and click "Free Check" to get instant results. We don't collect your email address, we don't store your search history, and we don't require registration. Your VIN check is anonymous and immediate. This is genuinely different from many "free" services that force you to create accounts or provide personal information before showing results.</p>

    <h3>What should I do if I find open recalls?</h3>
    <p>If your free VIN check reveals open recalls, contact an authorized dealer for your vehicle's manufacturer immediately to schedule a free repair. Recall repairs are always free regardless of vehicle age, mileage, or warranty status. Federal law requires manufacturers to fix recalled vehicles at no charge. Use our <a href="/recall-checker/">recall checker</a> to get detailed information about each recall campaign, including problem descriptions and remedy details. Don't ignore recalls—many involve serious safety defects like fire risks, brake failures, or airbag problems that could save your life if addressed.</p>
